# Copy to .env.local (never commit .env.local). All of this is optional for local development — with none of it set, a local server is open.

# Named accounts for the access gate (JSON on one line). The sign-in page then asks for a Login ID and password.
#   role "admin" can open the login dashboard at /admin; role "user" cannot. "title" is shown beside the name; "name" defaults to the id.
# Keep the real values out of the repository: set them in .env.local or, when hosting, as the (sensitive) SITE_USERS environment variable.
SITE_USERS='[{"id":"admin-id","password":"change-me","role":"admin"},{"id":"user-id","password":"change-me","role":"user","title":"Director"}]'

# Instead of accounts: one shared password (ignored when SITE_USERS is set)
# SITE_PASSWORD=

# Recommended with accounts: a long random string that signs the session cookie AND the sign-ins issued from the admin dashboard ("Add a user").
# Set it so editing SITE_USERS later does not end those sign-ins; changing it ends every session and every issued sign-in at once.
# (Without it, SITE_USERS itself is the secret.)
# GATE_SECRET=

# Optional: Login IDs whose issued sign-ins are switched off (comma-separated, takes effect on the next deployment). The server stores nothing about
# users added from the dashboard, so this is how you end one before its date.
# SITE_REVOKED=priya.sharma,sam

# Optional: be told when someone signs in with a USER account (never the admin's). One URL; the site POSTs one line — "Someone signed in to the prototype — <date and time>" —
# and nothing else is sent or stored. ntfy.sh (free, no account): https://ntfy.sh/<a-long-random-topic>, then subscribe to that topic in the ntfy app. Discord / Slack webhooks work too.
# LOGIN_NOTIFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh/nf-change-this-to-20-random-letters

# Optional: close the whole site after this moment, cookie or not
# SITE_EXPIRES=2026-11-15T18:00:00+05:30

# Optional: the public address, used for the link-preview image URL
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-domain.example

